WEBVTT

NOTE Source-backed English script captions. Global-ceil 25fps boundaries; ASR uncertainties are in audio-caption-manifest.json.

C01
00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.280
Offcut gives people and browser agents
the same cutting-stock workbench.

C02
00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:08.480
Fourteen parts fit;
the long board stays protected.

C03
00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:16.120
These illustrative measurements cover usable stock,
parts, kerf and the reusable-remnant threshold.

C04
00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:20.800
People own measurements and protection;
registered tools cannot change them.

C05
00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.080
The right-hand panel is
an external model recording harness,

C06
00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:27.600
not a chatbot inside the website.

C07
00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.680
A real model chooses native WebMCP calls

C08
00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.840
on the shared committed workbench.
This is not a ChatGPT recording.

C09
00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:40.280
The model compares three complete alternatives:

C10
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.360
less stock, fewer boards, or useful offcuts.

C11
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.000
The deterministic branch-and-bound solver checks quantities,
stock and one kerf per part.

C12
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.160
A search limit is not a proof of optimality.

C13
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.680
Less stock and fewer boards both open

C14
00:00:56.680 --> 00:00:59.600
seven point two metres across three boards:

C15
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.680
forty-two millimetres of sawdust,
plus one hundred fifty-eight millimetres of short scrap.

C16
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.960
Keeping useful offcuts opens
ten point zero eight metres across six boards:

C17
00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.560
forty-two millimetres of sawdust, no short scrap,

C18
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.840
and three point zero three eight metres reusable.

C19
00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.520
Computed trade-offs,
not measured time or carbon savings.

C20
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.520
The agent inspects and stages the exact plan.

C21
00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.240
Pause for review: this is not approval.

C22
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.240
Check stock identities, quantities and kerf.

C23
00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:35.480
No registered tool can approve.

C24
00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:39.680
These UI controls are automated,
not proof of human presence.

C25
00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:42.280
Now the controls check the acknowledgement.

C26
00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.080
Then they click Approve cut sheet.

C27
00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:50.320
The model inspects approval
and exports the actual CSV:

C28
00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:56.360
fourteen parts, part identifiers, offsets, kerf,
and the exact approved plan identifier.

C29
00:01:57.160 --> 00:01:59.200
Another draft cannot inherit approval.

C30
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.520
Typing four leaves committed kerf at three.

C31
00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:07.240
Pending edits block staging
without moving focus or saving.

C32
00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.800
Now commit: revision two invalidates the old approval.

C33
00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:12.560
The old plan is stale.

C34
00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:17.440
The model replans: fourteen parts,
fifty-six millimetres of sawdust.

C35
00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:19.960
Revision two is staged, not approved.

C36
00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:22.080
The acknowledgement stays unchecked.

C37
00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.400
Offcut itself needs no account,
model key or backend.

C38
00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.960
The workbench and MIT licensed source are public.

C39
00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.280
These are planning estimates, not machine instructions:

C40
00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:43.640
verify usable stock and safe cutting conditions at the bench.

